<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salar Jung I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1829">1829</a>–<a href="/years/1883">1883</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Mir Turab Ali Khan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Salar Jung I, born Mir Turab Ali Khan, was chief minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad-sindh">Hyderabad</a> State from 1853 until his death in 1883. He reorganised revenue collection, created modern district administration, courts, police and schools, and restored the state's finances. He kept Hyderabad loyal to the British during the rebellion of 1857 and acted as regent during the minority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahbub-ali-khan">Mahbub Ali Khan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salar-jung-i.jpg" alt="Salar Jung I" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: undated photo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Salar_Jung.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mir Turab Ali Khan was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> on 21 January 1829 into a family that had held office under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shahi-dynasty">Adil Shahi dynasty</a> of Bijapur, then under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> and finally under the Nizams. His father was Muhammad Ali Khan, eldest son of Mir Alam, and he was educated privately. In 1847 he served for eight months as talukdar of Medak, and in 1853 he succeeded his uncle Siraj-ul-Mulk as prime minister.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He took over a government with no separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>, in which everything lay in the minister's hands. He set up courts of justice in Hyderabad, organised a police force, repaired irrigation works and founded schools, among them Dar-ul-Ulum in 1854. In 1867 the state was divided into five divisions, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangabad">Aurangabad</a> and Hyderabad, and seventeen districts. He also set up a central mint, abolished the district mints and made the Hali Sikka rupee the standard currency.</p><p>During the rebellion of 1857 he warned the British of a planned attack on the Residency, which was repulsed, and used the government's preoccupation with the rising to push his reforms further. In 1868 he survived an attempt on his life on the day of Eid ul-Fitr. When Asaf Jah V died on 26 February 1869, he shared the regency for the two-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahbub-ali-khan">Mir Mahbub Ali Khan</a> with Shams-ul-Umara, and became sole regent on the latter's death in 1881.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He was made G.C.S.I. on 28 May 1870, and in 1876 he visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in an unsuccessful attempt to recover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a>, receiving an honorary D.C.L. from the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> on 21 June 1876. On his travels he bought the double sculpture Mephistopheles and Margaretta in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the marble Veiled Rebecca in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>; the collections of Salar Jung I, II and III later went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salar-jung-museum">Salar Jung Museum</a>.</p><p>He died of cholera at Hyderabad on 8 February 1883 and was buried at the Daira Mir Momin, and three days of mourning were declared across the state. His son Salar Jung II succeeded him as prime minister. Through his daughter Amat-uz-Zehra, who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahbub-ali-khan">Asaf Jah VI</a>, he was the maternal grandfather of the last Nizam, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-osman-ali-khan">Mir Osman Ali Khan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mir Turab Ali Khan, Salar Jung I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The most significant reformer was <b>Salar Jung I</b>, chief minister from 1853 to 1883, who reorganised revenue collection, created district administration on modern lines and founded new departments for justice, police and education.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hyderabad-state">Hyderabad State · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Vijayapura</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=16.82,75.72&amp;year=1864" aria-label="Vijayapura on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Salar Jung I"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3843 6044h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3843 6044h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=16.82,75.72&amp;year=1864">What was here in 1864? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
